working with u.s politics we're joined  now in the studio by labour's shadow  international trade secretary emily  thornbury thank you very much for being  with us  um we just heard sanji javid there  talking about donald trump damaging  democracy about the fragility of  democracy  what's your reaction to the fact that  the current president doesn't seem to be  accepting the result of the election i  don't think anybody can be surprised  i think that it's very much the sort of  pattern of behavior that he indulges in  and actually he's told us himself hasn't  he for some time that this is what he's  going to do  um i think what's important is that we  all stand united  those of us that are interested in  democracy and  supporters of western democracy in  particular this is the biggest uh  democracy in what in in the west um and  to have the sitting president saying  that the the result isn't valid  is is very damaging indeed and so i  think it's very important we all speak  with one voice on this  and we're quite clear that there has  been a  an election there has had a huge turnout  that all the votes have been  counted and president biden is president  biden  you of course haven't exactly held back  with your remarks about donald trump in  the past you've described him as racist  as a sexual predator you said he should  be treated like a bully i mean  it's not most destroyed an asteroid of  awfulness i think which was turned into  a cartoon in the united states  yes well that's what he is and i have  personally always thought  that the the government had a choice  when he was elected they could have  spent their time holding his hand and  sucking up to him and hoping to get  something  some crumbs from the table and how has  that worked we have got nothing from  that  you know we haven't got i mean they're  still putting tariffs  on whiskey you know because of a  long-standing dispute on  aircraft you know we're supposed to be  doing a trade deal and yet we can't even  get these punitive tariffs off  we can't even get the killer of poor  harry dunn  you know back to britain in order to  face our justice  you know all of these things that we  knew the prime minister has gone  personally to his good friend  donald trump and asked for these things  why do you have gotten a good friend  and why do you think the government  sucked up to him well i think what they  have done  i i think they genuinely what is the  evidence for that what they sacked up to  him  that they're good friends well they held  his hand you know the prime minister  held his hand on a number of a  previous prime minister we've heard  we've we've seen the way in which  in which uh they have never criticized  they have never said  that they disagreed with him even during  the election itself we've heard them  being merely mouthed  and not set and not standing up but  isn't that it's a democratic election  isn't it yes  you wouldn't expect people to be to be  criticizing a candidate while a  democratic election is going on with  none by most important allies also if  you look at  for example in policy terms you know  that there's quite a lot of clear water  between boris johnson and donald trump  isn't there on things  like trade on things like iran or things  like climate change  okay so where do you want me to start so  let's start with yes i understand during  an election you know it would be wrong  for foreign powers to be intervening for  heaven's sake  but once the election has finished and  once the votes are being counted and you  have the president of the united states  saying that the visit  saying the vote is not legitimate then i  think that  that our prime minister should have  stood up along with the german foreign  minister  along with the irish prime minister and  all kinds of international figures and  said  this is not the behavior that we expect  this is wrong so i think that's that  i think that there there of course there  are some policy differences  uh between britain and the united states  and thankfully now  we are going to have a president who  will be more in line with our  policies and with what we think is the  right way of behaving  but there has been a general approach a  populist approach  from boris johnson which i think he has  had in common with donald trump and that  is why they have  you know they have had such a close  friendship that's why they have been so  mutually congratulatory  you know and it'll be very difficult i  think it's not impossible but it'll be  very difficult to move from that to  being close to joe biden i understand  that  do you think that obviously you know  there are many  valid criticisms that people make about  donald trump but at the same time you  know he has  when you look at international affairs  he's reset the relationship with china  he's  tried to make approaches to north korea  and  he's also of course not started any wars  like some other u.s presidents that you  could think of has he really been so bad  i think that he has been a disrupter i  think that he  for example on north korea what does he  do he marches in there  he says he's going to he's going to sort  things out he he brings us to the edge  of war  do you remember how anxious we all were  as to what an earth was going to happen  next  and then he pulls back and thinks that  it can all be done by way of a  friendship and yet nothing has been  achieved  and look at the amount of time that  we've wasted and what are the north  koreans been doing in the meantime  we have seen the way in which he  withdrew support from the kurds  who were you know working with the west  um  you know and and and just basically left  them to their fate  uh he has been a disrupter he has  undermined international institutions  look what he's done to the who  look at the fact that we keep talking  about the world trade organization the  world trade organization  doesn't have a court that properly works  because the appeal court  doesn't have enough judges because  donald trump keeps  saying he's not prepared to have the  judges whose names are put forward so we  don't have an appeal court on the wto  so the conservatives say oh it's fine we  can go  into a world you know run by the run by  the world trade organization and yet the  world trade organization is  fundamentally disrupt you know  dysfunctional because of the behavior of  donald trump amongst other things  no he has not been a good thing for the  international  world you know rules-based order and now  we have with joe biden somebody  who does understand that we need to work  together it is not a question of america  first  it's a question of the world first and  the only way that's going to work is if  we work together  you um clearly much happier with the  idea of abide in presidency and a trump  presidency i don't think that would  surprise anybody and but i just wonder  with your  shadow international trade secretary hat  on are you concerned  that actually joe biden is going to be  tougher to do a trade deal with  post-brexit than donald trump who said  that because of the special relationship  britain would be at the front of the  queue  so i think that what we have to do is we  have to kind of unpick this a little bit  because donald trump and and boris  johnson may have been talking about a  trade deal  but there have been all kinds of  problems in reality with trying to get a  trade deal together so  if we talk about you know agriculture  then when we have looked at it we have  realized that we haven't wanted to have  agricultural products come into britain  that are produced in a completely  different way but do you think joey  barnett will be easier no no all i'm  saying is that the reality is  is that actually getting a fun a an  all-singing all-dancing trade deal is  something that takes  many years and is quite you know quite  complex and quite difficult to  to put together i think i think that the  right approach would be this  would be for us to say yes of course we  want to increase trade  and one of the things that we now have  in common with the with the new  president  is our commitment to climate change now  we should be  working with them in order to be able to  to  tackle climate change together and  actually there are all sorts of  opportunities in terms of  of jobs and growth in that let's work  together on that  um but let's also then as the next thing  is make sure that we have a proper deal  with the european union  with whom we do half of our trade and  that we're not undermining the good  friday agreement  and then we can move on to working with  the united states further on  other areas where we could increase  trade at the moment we do 18  of our trade with the united states we  do 48 of our trade with the european  union we have to kind of keep these  things  in that sort of perspective okay and now  while i've got  you i'm keen to talk to you about jeremy  corbyn being suspended from  the labour party for saying that  allegations of anti-semitism were  overstated of course it comes after that  report by the  equality and human rights watchdog that  said that labour had acted unlawfully  have you spoken to him since then i  haven't spoken to jeremy i'm  i'm probably going to be seeing him  today obviously because it's remembrance  sunday  but no i have not spoken to him directly  you of course were in the shadow cabinet  with jeremy corbyn a time when you were  all going around knocking on doors  giving interviews saying that he should  be in number 10.  and now you're saying it's right for him  to be suspended from  labour do you regret being in the shadow  cabinet with him well  the first thing is is that i am not  expressing an opinion about  with the disciplinary process in  relation to jeremy because it has always  been my  firmly held view that it is not for  politicians to make decisions on this  front what we have always been told  is that it should be done by the party  or it should be done on an arm's length  basis  the the ehrc report has also  specifically said that politicians  should keep out of this  and that it should be done in an  independent way so whatever my private  views are about this i'm not going to  express them  on national television okay but do you  regret being in the shadow cabinet  campaigning for jeremy corbyn to be  prime minister when at the time we knew  about  allegations of the way labour was  handling anti-semitism  so i think that i it's quite clear i'm  on the record i was on record at  party conference in the media and i  think that some of these  uh books that have been written about  what was going on behind the scenes  make it clear that i was saying in  private what i was saying in public so  if you thought  that then why why were you staying in  the shadow cabinet because  because i felt that it was a struggle  but that we were going in the right  direction  so that you know the processes had taken  too long to improve  but they were getting better i didn't  know the extent  of the issues in terms of the  interference of jeremy's office  with the disciplinary process i didn't  know the extent of that because i'm  obviously i'm not on the nec  okay i'm not involved in the background  stuff but i knew that we were making  progress  it was a struggle okay but it was the  right place for me to be was to be there  making sure that we were going in the  right direction uh we're at time but i  just want to squeeze one last thought in  we're in coronavirus lockdown yeah if  you knew your neighbours were breaking  the rules having some people over for  dinner would you report them to police  no but i would talk to them about it  okay fair enough  emily thornbury thank you very much for  being on the programme today thank you

